<%--
Quick_Start_Guide - Ouro setup and core language chapters.
--%>
<Define:Quick_Start_Guide>
  <section
    id="getting-started"
    class="docs-section"
    data-search-section
    aria-labelledby="getting-started-title"
  >
    <h2 id="getting-started-title">Getting Started</h2>
    <p>
      Ouro currently requires Python 3, a POSIX-compatible shell, and a C compiler available as
      <code>cc</code> or <code>gcc</code>. On Windows, use WSL or Git Bash.
    </p>
    <pre class="docs-code" data-language="terminal"><code>git clone https://github.com/eluvane/ouro.git
cd ouro
sh scripts/bootstrap.sh
sh scripts/ouro1.sh check samples/tutorial/02_nat.ouro</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section
    id="language-syntax"
    class="docs-section"
    data-search-section
    aria-labelledby="language-syntax-title"
  >
    <h2 id="language-syntax-title">Language Syntax</h2>
    <p>
      Definitions name a term and its type. Applications are whitespace-separated, line comments
      start with <code>--</code>, and top-level declarations end with a semicolon.
    </p>
    <pre class="docs-code"><code>def id (A : Type) (x : A) : A := x;

def message : String := "hello";</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section
    id="dependent-functions"
    class="docs-section"
    data-search-section
    aria-labelledby="dependent-functions-title"
  >
    <h2 id="dependent-functions-title">
      Dependent Functions
    </h2>
    <p>
      A named function binder lets the result type depend on the input value. Elaboration is kept
      explicit and bounded rather than hidden behind broad inference.
    </p>
    <pre class="docs-code"><code>def apply (A : Type) (B : A -> Type)
          (f : (x : A) -> B x) (x : A) : B x :=
  f x;</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section
    id="inductive-data"
    class="docs-section"
    data-search-section
    aria-labelledby="inductive-data-title"
  >
    <h2 id="inductive-data-title">Inductive Data</h2>
    <p>
      Inductive declarations define data through named constructors. Natural-number literals
      elaborate to the current <code>Nat</code> representation.
    </p>
    <pre class="docs-code"><code>inductive Nat : Type :=
  | Z : Nat
  | S : Nat -> Nat;

def two : Nat := 2;</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section
    id="pattern-matching"
    class="docs-section"
    data-search-section
    aria-labelledby="pattern-matching-title"
  >
    <h2 id="pattern-matching-title">Pattern Matching</h2>
    <p>
      Matches are constructor-based. Recursive definitions use an explicit <code>fix</code>; the
      logical core supports structural recursion rather than unrestricted recursion.
    </p>
    <pre class="docs-code"><code>def pred (n : Nat) : Nat :=
  match n with
  | Z => Z
  | S k => k
  end;</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section
    id="modules-imports"
    class="docs-section"
    data-search-section
    aria-labelledby="modules-imports-title"
  >
    <h2 id="modules-imports-title">Modules & Imports</h2>
    <p>
      Every <code>.ouro</code> file is a module. Imports are relative to the importing file, and an
      optional alias qualifies imported names inside the current flat namespace model.
    </p>
    <pre class="docs-code"><code>import "../std/list.ouro";
import "nat_lib.ouro" as N;

def two : N.Nat := N.S N.one;</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section
    id="effects-io"
    class="docs-section"
    data-search-section
    aria-labelledby="effects-io-title"
  >
    <h2 id="effects-io-title">Effects & IO</h2>
    <p>
      Runnable programs export <code>main : IO Unit</code>. Host IO remains outside the logical
      kernel, while the current <code>do</code> subset provides sequencing and <code>let!</code>.
    </p>
    <pre class="docs-code"><code>import "../../std/io.ouro";

-- @entry main
def main : IO Unit :=
  do println "hello";
     exit 0</code></pre>
  </section>
</Define:Quick_Start_Guide>
